{"records":[{"id":"54v7hn8hvz","title":"Legionella pneumophila exploits the endo-lysosomal network for phagosome biogenesis","description":"L. pneumophila strains harboring wild-type rpsL such as Lp02rpsLWT cannot replicate in mouse bone marrow-derived macrophages (BMDMs) due to induction of extensive lysosome damage and apoptosis. The mechanism of this unique infection-induced cell death remains unknown. Using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screening, we identified Hmg20a and Nol9 as host factors important for restricting strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Depletion of Hmg20a protects macrophages from infection-induced lysosomal damage and apoptosis, allowing productive bacterial replication. The restriction imposed by Hmg20a was mediated by repressing the expression of several endo-lysosomal proteins, including the small GTPase Rab7. We found that SUMOylated Rab7 is recruited to the bacterial phagosome via SulF, a Dot/Icm effector that harbors a SUMO-interacting motif (SIM). Moreover, overexpression of Rab7 rescues intracellular growth of strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Our results establish that L. pneumophila exploits the lysosomal network for the biogenesis of its phagosome in BMDMs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-07T16:49:43.441Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54v7hn8hvz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54v7hn8hvz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chuang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"75brhkh25r","title":"A genome-wide hepatocyte CRISPR-CAS9 screen to identify host factors essential for optimal Plasmodium liver stage development. ","description":"To identify host genes critical for Plasmodium LS development, we used the GeCKOv2 sgRNA pool to generate a whole-genome knockout library in HepG2-CD81 cells (Sanjana et al., 2014; Shalem et al., 2014). HepG2-CD81 cells were transduced with lentivirus containing the pooled GeCKOv2 sgRNA library of 114,123 sgRNAs targeting 19,031 protein-coding genes (~6 sgRNAs/gene) and selected in puromycin for 5-7 days. To evaluate sgRNA diversity in the HepG2-CD81-GeCKOv2 library, we PCR-amplified the integrated sgRNA cassettes from genomic DNA extracted from transduced cells and subjected the amplified library to Illumina sequencing. At the gene level, sgRNAs targeting all but 15 of 19,031 (99.92%) protein-coding genes were observed. Twelve to fourteen days after post-transduction, we infected forty million puromycin-resistant cells with a green fluorescent protein (GFP) expressing-Plasmodium yoelii at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 0.3. After 24 h of infection, cells were sorted into infected and bystander cell populations by GFP signal intensity with fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS). Separately, a parallel culture of uninfected cells was also maintained to normalize the sgRNA frequency distributions. We obtained four independent biological replicates with library generation and sequencing occurring in parallel. Genes with significantly enriched sgRNAs were identified for both the bystander and infected populations when compared to uninfected cells. Cells that harbor genetic alterations restricting P. yoelii development (i.e., sgRNAs that target host genes important for infection) were expected to be enriched in the uninfected bin; we termed this group ‘putative positive regulators of infection’. We categorized sgRNAs enriched in the infected cells as ‘putative negative regulators of infection’. In this initial screen, we identified 242 sgRNAs that were statistically enriched in infected or bystander groups after accounting for multiple hypotheses. There were 67 sgRNAs significantly enriched in the infected cells compared to uninfected cells and 175 genes were significantly enriched in the bystander bin relative to the uninfected bin. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-27T07:31:54.012Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/75brhkh25r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/75brhkh25r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamalakannan","last_name":"Vijayan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nadia","last_name":"Arang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Morrison","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rechel ","last_name":"Geiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachael ","last_name":"Parks","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam J ","last_name":"Lewis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fred D ","last_name":"Mast ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alyse N ","last_name":"Douglass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heather S ","last_name":"Kain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John D","last_name":"Aitchison ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarrod S ","last_name":"Johnson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alan ","last_name":"Aderem","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Kaushansky","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sgdcys9b82","title":"Mycobacterium-Host Screening Identifies Novel Lysosomal Escape Pathways","description":"This dataset explores intracellular survival mechanisms of Mycobacterium leprae. Using clinical lesion proteomics, we identified 14 highly expressed T7SS effectors. Genome-wide yeast two-/three-hybrid screening against a macrophage interactome revealed 132 host targets and mapped the first global M. leprae effector–host interaction network. Functional validation defined two conserved lysosomal escape pathways: PPE68–Coro1A preventing phagosome–lysosome fusion, and EsxA–EsxB–CCDC115 disrupting V-ATPase assembly. Comparative M. marinum models confirmed their essential role in survival and pathogenesis. The dataset includes proteomic profiles, interaction matrices, and validation assays, which can be used to investigate host–pathogen interactions and identify therapeutic targets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-29T07:59:18.944Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sgdcys9b82","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sgdcys9b82"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bingqing","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"xiaojie","last_name":"Liao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongyu","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Longyun ","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rongxian","last_name":"Xie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haihong","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuzhen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoyu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nannan","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"玮玮","last_name":"王","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhongrui","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yinjie","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peidian","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenchao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanqiang","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenzhen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xuechao","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianwen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhe","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zihao","last_name":"Mi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Furen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Bacterial Pathogenesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gtswbcb5nv","title":"Compartmentalization-Aided Interaction Screening Reveals Extensive High-Order Complexes within the SARS-CoV-2 Proteome","description":"Bearing a relatively large single-stranded RNA genome in nature, SARS-CoV-2 utilizes sophisticated replication/transcription complexes (RTCs), mainly composed of a network of nonstructural proteins and nucleocapsid protein, to establish efficient infection. Here, we develop an innovative interaction screening strategy based on phase separation in cellulo, namely compartmentalization of protein-protein interactions in cells (CoPIC). Utilizing CoPIC screening, we map the interaction network among RTC-related viral proteins. We identify a total of 47 binary interactions among 14 proteins governing replication, discontinuous transcription, and translation of coronaviruses. Further exploration via CoPIC leads to the discovery of extensive ternary complexes composed of these components, which infer potential higher-order complexes. Taken together, our results present an efficient, and robust interaction screening strategy, and indicate the existence of a complex interaction network among RTC-related factors, thus opening up opportunities to understand SARS-CoV-2 biology and develop therapeutic interventions for COVID-19.\n\nThe uploaded files are raw data of microscopy images in this paper.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-09T21:02:03.928Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gtswbcb5nv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gtswbcb5nv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weifan","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gaofeng","last_name":"Pei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hongrui","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaohui","last_name":"Ju","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jing","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiang","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pilong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","Protein-Protein Interaction Networks","New Technology","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4224kkbrry","title":"Analysis scripts for the paper: The Skin-Brain Axis: Shared Genetic Architecture Between Immune-Mediated Skin Diseases and Emotional/Behavioral Disorders","description":"STAR★METHODS\n\tGWAS Data Sources\n\tGlobal and Local Genetic Correlation Analysis \n\tMulti-trait Association Analysis\n\tFunctional Annotation\n\tGene Mapping Analysis\n\tGenome-Wide Gene-based and Gene-set Analyses\n\tTranscriptome-Wide Association Analysis \n\tLinkage Disequilibrium Score Regression Applied to Specifically Expressed Genes (LDSC-SEG)\n\tTissue and Cell Type Specificity Analysis\n\tMendelian Randomization to Identify Potential Causal Plasma Protein Targets\n\tSoftware\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-30T15:12:57.987Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4224kkbrry","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4224kkbrry"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yunxuan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Code Reuse"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kbr33hs2m8","title":"Immunogenicity and reactogenicity of heterologous immunization schedules using Sputnik V, ChAdOx1-S, BBIBP-CorV, Ad5-nCoV and mRNA-1273","description":"Heterologous vaccination regimens against COVID-19 provide a rational strategy to rapidly increase the rate of coverage in many regions of the world. In Argentina, a number of different\n vaccine platforms are being used, including the non-replicating adenovirus vaccines ChAdOx1-S, Ad5-nCoV, and Sputnik V; the mRNA vaccines BNT162b2 and mRNA-1273; and the\n inactivated SARS-CoV-2 vaccine BBIBP-CorV. Although data are available about mRNA and ChAdOx1 vaccines combination, very limited information has been reported combining these\n platforms with other vaccines widely used in developing countries, such as BBIBP-CorV and Sputnik V. We assessed the immunogenicity and reactogenicity of 15 vaccine combinations in\n a cohort of 1314 participants. The antibody response was evaluated by determining IgG antibodies anti-spike and serum neutralizing titers. We observed that a number of heterologous\n vaccine combinations are equivalent or superior to the homologous schemes. For all the cohorts analyzed, the highest antibody response was induced by mRNA-1273 as second dose. The\n results also highlight the benefit of using heterologous schedules in individuals primed with BBIBP-CorV. No serious adverse events were detected in any of the schedules analyzed. Our\n observations provide rational support for the use of different vaccine combinations with the aim of achieving wide vaccine coverage in the shortest possible time.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-27T16:06:16.986Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kbr33hs2m8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kbr33hs2m8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carla","last_name":"Pacuale","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Augusto","last_name":"Varese","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ojeda","last_name":"Diego","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pamela","last_name":"Rodríguez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Esteban","last_name":"Miglietta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Santiago","last_name":"Oviedo Rouco","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María Mora","last_name":"Gonzalez Lopez Ledesma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andres","last_name":"Rossi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Gamarnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vaccine","Immunization","Neutralizing Antibody","Humoral Immunity","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19","Omicron Coronavirus Variant"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v4zbbbsr5t","title":"VPS33A and VPS18 orchestrate porcine epidemic diarrhea virus replication by modulating autophagic flux","description":"Completion of autophagy through autophagosome-lysosome fusion represents a critical regulatory checkpoint that is frequently manipulated during viral infection, yet how coronaviruses exploit this terminal step remains incompletely understood. Porcine epidemic diarrhea virus (PEDV), a highly pathogenic alphacoronavirus, has been reported to induce autophagy, but whether and how complete autophagic flux contributes to viral replication is unclear. Here, using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screen in Vero cells, we identify the HOPS complex subunits VPS33A and VPS18 as essential host factors for PEDV infection. Genetic ablation of either VPS33A or VPS18 profoundly suppresses viral progeny production and arrests autophagic flux at the autophagosome stage, demonstrating that PEDV replication is associated with HOPS-mediated autolysosome formation. Domain-mapping analyses further reveal that the structural integrity of VPS33A and the α-solenoid plus RING domains of VPS18 are indispensable for both autophagosome-lysosome fusion and efficient viral replication. Mechanistically, we show that PEDV non-structural proteins nsp3 and nsp4 physically interact with VPS33A and VPS18. While either protein alone initiates autophagosome formation, their coordinated action contributes to drive complete autophagy in a HOPS-dependent manner. Collectively, our findings uncover a coronavirus strategy that actively promotes autophagic completion through VPS33A and VPS18, two core subunits of the HOPS complex, to support replication, thereby establishing autolysosome formation as a critical host process exploited by PEDV.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-18T15:12:38.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v4zbbbsr5t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v4zbbbsr5t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"liang","last_name":"guo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"the National Key Research and Development Program of China","grant_id":"2023YFC3404301, 2023YFF1000204, and 2021YFA0805901"},{"id":null,"name":"the Hainan Provincial Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"323QN261"}],"categories":["Virus","Animal"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9bzr2sykrs","title":"De Novo Transcriptome Assembly and Data for the Blue-winged Teal (Anas discors)","description":"The blue-winged teal (Anas discors) is a recreationally and ecologically important dabbling duck species in North America. Transcriptomic data of this species can be used in public and animal health studies given its role as a natural reservoir host for avian influenza, which can be a zoonotic disease of high concern. Ileum and bursa of Fabricius tissues were sampled from six captive raised blue-winged teals, four of the six who were experimentally infected with low-pathogenic avian influenza virus H5N9. RNAseq data were generated from extracted total mRNA from each tissue and pooled to create a de novo assembly of the transcriptome using Trinity. A total of 571,105 transcripts were identified at 449,956 unique unigenes that have been functionally annotated. This transcriptome will be useful for future blue-winged teal gene expression research, especially in hypothesis driven differential expression studies to determine the driving forces of avian influenza host-pathogen interactions, spatial distribution, and transmission.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-11-15T16:28:48.346Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9bzr2sykrs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9bzr2sykrs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Amanda","last_name":"Dolinski","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jarred","last_name":"Homola","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jen","last_name":"Owen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mark ","last_name":"Jankowski","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Transcriptome"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140083,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":149,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140083,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139263,"restricted":71,"embargoed":749},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2309},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":699},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":475},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":445},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":421},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":400},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":340},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":276},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":236}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137420},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1914},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":537},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":372},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":359},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":328}]}}